// Title: Service Security Descriptor Tampering Via Sc.EXE
// Sigma ID: 98c5aeef-32d5-492f-b174-64a691896d25
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2023-02-28 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: persistence, privilege-escalation, execution, stealth, T1574.011
// Description: Detection of sc.exe utility adding a new service with special permission which hides that
//   service.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://blog.talosintelligence.com/2021/10/threat-hunting-in-large-datasets-by.html
//   - https://www.sans.org/blog/red-team-tactics-hiding-windows-services/
//   - https://twitter.com/Alh4zr3d/status/1580925761996828672
//   - https://twitter.com/0gtweet/status/1628720819537936386
//   - https://itconnect.uw.edu/tools-services-support/it-systems-infrastructure/msinf/other-help/understanding-sddl-syntax/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_sc_sdset_modification.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\sc.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "sc.exe") and ProcessCommandLine contains "sdset"
